<section class="bac-coda" id="coda">
    <div class="content-area">
        <p class="bac-codabig">For four thousand years, the pledge was a promise.<br>Bitcoin is the first collateral where the pledge is a <em>verifiable fact</em>.</p>
        <div class="bac-prose">
            <p>Gold never escaped its trade-off: a bearer asset too cumbersome to pledge at scale, or a practical paper claim that quietly reintroduced every trust failure from the goldsmiths to the salad-oil tanks. Bitcoin is the first asset that is both at once. It settles bearer-final within the hour &ndash; six <span class="bac-term">confirmations</span><span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Blocks added to the chain after the one containing your transaction, arriving roughly every ten minutes; six is the customary standard for finality.</span></span>, roughly ten minutes apart, is the customary standard &ndash; it divides to eight decimals, and it is verifiable by anyone, anywhere, without permission. A lender can control collateral they cannot steal; a borrower can watch a pledge they no longer hold. No vault, registry, or receipt in the history of secured credit has offered that combination.</p>
            <p>The claim stops there, and deliberately. This is a capability, not yet the practice. Most institutional bitcoin sits in omnibus custodial accounts, holding the asset in precisely the paper mode it was designed to escape, and lenders price bitcoin collateral accordingly, as The Gap shows in numbers. Whether the gap closes is not asserted here. It is instrumented: the tripwires are named, and <a href="#" data-goto="practice">the record in The Practice tab</a> logs what happens.</p>
            <p>One thing follows for the patient reader, stated plainly. Collateral value is a use of bitcoin, and today&rsquo;s lending terms show the market barely prices it. If that changes &ndash; if the properties measured above start showing up in rates and loan-to-values &ndash; the benefit lands on whoever already holds the asset when it does. You do not need to borrow against bitcoin today for this to matter: holding it is what keeps the option open. This page does not predict that the option pays. It records, quarterly, whether it has begun to.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

